      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "A selective ankle block, blocking the tibial, deep and superficial peroneal nerves, can be used successfully for great toe surgery. No comparative information is available on selective ankle block using ropivacaine and levobupivacaine."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We compared the onset time and success rate of a selective ankle block using low volumes (12 ml) of ropivacaine 10 mg/ml and levobupivacaine 7.5 mg/ml in 40 patients undergoing elective repair of bilateral hallux valgus. Each patient received an ankle block induced in one foot with ropivacaine and in the contralateral foot with levobupivacaine."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The success rate was higher [90% vs. 75%, hazard ratio (95% CI) 0.39 (0.23-0.64)] and anesthesia onset time was shorter (median, 10 vs. 20 min) after ropivacaine than after levobupivacaine. In successful ankle blocks, post-operative pain was similar in the two groups."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "In this study, ropivacaine 10 mg/ml had a shorter anesthesia onset time and a higher success rate than levobupivacaine 7.5 mg/ml for selective ankle block."
        }
      ]},
